fpac (4.1.6-rc7)

	* fpacwpd: the White Pages record of a node was never updated by
	  a neighbour once it existed. init_client() (wp/fpacwpd.c) typed a
	  session as a peer WP server only when the remote callsign read
	  "WP-0", but ax25_ntoa() does not print a -0 SSID and returns
	  "WP". Every session between two nodes was therefore handled as
	  an ordinary user: db_set() was called without force and refused
	  to update a node record, and broadcast_dirty() never relayed a
	  changed record to the other neighbours. The stale record then
	  aged until wpmaint marked it deleted, and the node could no
	  longer be reached by "C CALLSIGN". Both spellings are now
	  accepted (is_wp_server_call).
	* fpacwpd: as a peer is now recognised, only one session is kept
	  with each neighbour (there were two, one in each direction), and
	  a record received from a neighbour is no longer answered with a
	  wp_type_response meant for user clients.
	* fpacwpd: the right to force a record, meant for wpedit, was held
	  in a global flag set by the first WPEDIT connection and never
	  cleared; every client then kept it until fpacwpd was restarted.
	  It is now tested on the session that sends the record.
	* fpacwpd: a vector exchange is chained to the previous one as long
	  as the vectors differ (end_transaction from the called side, then
	  a new vector request from the caller). Nothing bounded these
	  rounds: when the difference cannot be resolved, for instance with
	  a neighbour on an older version that refuses node records, they
	  could follow each other without a pause. At most three chained
	  rounds are now allowed per hour with a neighbour, then fpacwpd
	  waits for the hourly exchange and logs "Vectors still differ with
	  adjacent" (vector_round_allowed, WPA_VECTOR_MAX_ROUNDS).
	* fpacwpd: a record received from a neighbour with the same date
	  as the local one but a different content replaced it and was
	  broadcast again, so two versions of a record could replace each
	  other endlessly around a ring of WP servers. At equal dates only
	  the higher version is now taken, compared on the fields carried
	  by the protocol and in the same way on every node; a deleted
	  record ranks above a valid one. A record edited with wpedit is
	  still always applied. The local deletion date of a record
	  received again unchanged is no longer cleared (wp/db.c,
	  wp_wire_cmp, db_set).
	* fpacnode, "Routes" command: the ROSE routes are listed by
	  increasing address instead of kernel order (decreasing mask),
	  which scattered the routes of one DNIC across the list. Each
	  DNIC now starts with its DNIC-wide route, followed by its
	  addresses in increasing order. Only the listing is sorted: the
	  kernel still tries the most specific route first
	  (node/command.c, sort_rs_nodes).
	* fpacnode, "Nodes" and "Users" commands: the headings said
	  something else than what is listed. "Nodes" lists the callsign
	  users connect to, not the callsign a node uses on its links: its
	  heading is now "FPAC Nodes - callsigns to connect to". "Users"
	  lists every AX.25 link of the node, most of them links with the
	  other nodes, then the ROSE circuits: its three headings are now
	  "AX.25 Level 2 sessions - users and links between nodes", "ROSE
	  Level 3 sessions" and "ROSE Level 3 transits". Only the headings
	  change; help/Nodes.hlp, which still described NET/ROM nodes, and
	  help/Users.hlp are rewritten.
	* fpacnode: new "DNic" command (DN). It lists the DNIC of the
	  node and the DNICs found in fpac.routes with the name of their
	  country, read from itu.dnic; "DN 5050" gives the country of
	  any DNIC (node/command.c, do_dnic; help/DNic.hlp).

fpac (4.1.6-rc6)

	* fpad: every time fpad stopped (FPAC stopped or restarted), the
	  kernel was given an arbitrary ROSE level 2 callsign. Until fpad
	  was running again, the ROSE frames of the node went out with that
	  callsign as their source and opened ghost AX.25 links to the
	  neighbours, visible in the "u" command with an unreadable callsign
	  (for example ")0=|U}-10") or "*". In clear_nodes() (pad/fpad.c),
	  the call meant to READ the node callsign used SIOCRSSL2CALL, which
	  WRITES it, with an uninitialised variable. It now uses
	  SIOCRSGL2CALL.

fpac (4.1.6-rc5)

	* fpacnode as a gateway: a long output seen through two nodes (for
	  example "u" on a remote node) arrived with holes, or without its
	  final prompt, which looked like a "frozen" display. When the link
	  to the user was full, the write was refused (EAGAIN) and usflush()
	  (node/io.c) threw away the current block, up to 251 characters,
	  without reporting anything. usflush() now waits for the link to
	  drain and retries; it only gives up on a real error or after 120
	  seconds.
	* "C CALLSIGN": an FPAC node known to the White Pages is reached
	  over ROSE, even if a NetRom node has the same callsign ("c
	  f6bvp-12" went out over NetRom when the node uses its own callsign
	  for NetRom). The NetRom alias and the nodes absent from the White
	  Pages still go out over NetRom (node/gateway.c, is_wp_node).
	* The message "No NetRom node ... (NetRom nodes with this callsign:
	  ...)" is no longer displayed before a successful ROSE connection,
	  where it read like a failed NetRom attempt: it is only displayed
	  if the callsign was found nowhere.

fpac (4.1.6-rc4)

	* Callsigns without SSID: by AX.25 convention, a callsign written
	  without an SSID is the callsign with SSID 0 (F3KT = F3KT-0). FPAC
	  compared them as plain text, whereas the kernel always writes "-0"
	  (/proc/net/...), the libax25 library never writes it, and
	  fpac.conf or the user may do either: "F3KT" and "F3KT-0" were then
	  different. A common function (callsign_eq) now compares callsigns
	  according to the convention. Consequences fixed:
	  - "C CALLSIGN" to a station heard directly (mheard file) never
	    found a station with SSID 0, whether F4JQC or F4JQC-0 was typed
	    (fpacnode and flexd);
	  - fpad never routed an incoming ROSE call to an application
	    declared without an SSID, and over AX.25 "-0" had to be typed;
	  - fpac.conf aliases: "F3KT" and "F3KT-0" designate the same alias
	    (it is no longer necessary to declare both);
	  - recognition of the node's own callsign, exclusion of the failed
	    neighbour during the ROSE failover, route management command,
	    filters of the list commands and fpacstat statistics.
	* NetRom connection: the callsign typed was compared by prefix with
	  the NetRom nodes, and NetRom is tried before the White Pages: "c
	  f6bvp-1" (the BBS, present in the White Pages) went to the NetRom
	  node F6BVP-12, and "c f3kt" to the first F3KT-x node of the table.
	  The match is now exact (callsign, with F6BVP = F6BVP-0, or alias).
	  When no node matches exactly, a help message lists the NetRom
	  nodes that have the same callsign with another SSID, without
	  connecting to them, and the search goes on (White Pages, heard
	  stations...).

fpac (4.1.6-rc3)

	* fpacnode "r" (Routes) command: the exact ten-digit routes, that
	  is the routes to adjacent nodes (for example 2080,817501 via
	  F5ZHO-9 on F3KT), were displayed nowhere: the command skipped them
	  on purpose. They are now listed with the other routes, in kernel
	  order (most specific first).
	* "r <address>" only displayed the general routes covering the
	  address, for example route 2080 via F6BVP-9, whereas the kernel
	  uses the exact route to F5ZHO-9. The command now lists all the
	  routes covering the address and shows on the last line the route
	  actually used to connect to it ("Route used to ... via ..."),
	  with the same rule as the kernel and as the "Relaying via" message
	  of the connection. "r <DNIC>" lists all the routes of that DNIC,
	  including the exact routes.
	* ROSE connection: the "Relaying via ..." message and the automatic
	  failover after a failure walked the ROSE routes in the reverse
	  order of the kernel's. The libax25 library reads
	  /proc/net/rose_nodes adding each line at the head of the list,
	  whereas the kernel orders the routes from the most specific to the
	  most general and takes the first one that fits. When a specific
	  route existed (for example 2080,175 via F6BVP-9) next to the
	  general DNIC route (2080 via F6BVP-11), FPAC announced the
	  neighbour of the general route while the kernel used the one of
	  the specific route, and the failover could remove the route of the
	  wrong neighbour. The routes are now read in kernel order.
	* "r" command: new "Connect" column with the callsign to use to
	  connect to the node (its L2call, for example F6BVP-8), for each
	  exact route to an adjacent node. The routing columns only display
	  the level 3 callsigns of the neighbours (for example F6BVP-9),
	  which are used for routing but not for a user connection. The
	  callsign is looked up by address in the White Pages; "?" if it is
	  not there. "r <address>" adds the line "To connect : C
	  <callsign>".
	* "n" (Nodes) command: the "Callsign" title becomes "Connect",
	  since these are the connect callsigns of the nodes.
	* fpacwpd: if a White Pages client disappeared during a reply
	  (fpacnode session cut, for example), the libwp library closed the
	  socket behind fpacwpd's back. fpacwpd kept it in its wait list and
	  select() failed in a loop: 100 % CPU and White Pages blocked until
	  FPAC was restarted. The library no longer closes the caller's
	  socket, and fpacwpd removes from its list any descriptor already
	  closed instead of looping.
	* White Pages: a connection under a callsign that has a node record
	  moved the address of that record to the node where the connection
	  took place (wp_update_addr). The result was a "node" whose address
	  changed at every connection and differed from one node to another
	  (F6BVP-0 record, displayed by "n" and by the Connect column). A
	  node record is no longer modified by connections: only the node
	  itself announces its address. A deleted node record brought back
	  to life by a connection becomes a user record.
	* fpacwpd: a White Pages synchronisation neighbour could stay in
	  "connection in progress" indefinitely (seen on F3KT for 6 of its
	  7 neighbours, including F5ZHO): when the socket had been closed by
	  libwp behind its back and its number was reassigned to another
	  client, the connection guard timer no longer reset the neighbour,
	  and the synchronisation with that node never resumed without
	  restarting fpacwpd. The neighbour is now reset in that case, and a
	  reused socket number first frees the old context.
	* fpacwpd: crash (segmentation fault in count_dirty_context) and
	  synchronisation neighbours stuck in "connection in progress".
	  fpacwpd only recognises a synchronisation peer if its callsign is
	  written "WP-0", but the AX.25 library never writes the -0 suffix:
	  every synchronisation connection was handled as a plain client,
	  and closing it did not detach the neighbour, which kept a
	  reference to a freed context. Closing now always detaches the
	  neighbour concerned, and the count of records to send ignores a
	  freed context.
	* fpacnode "w" (White Pages) command: it only requested 20 records,
	  sorted by address, and therefore looked incomplete and out of
	  order. It now displays all the records (200 at most), from the
	  most recent to the oldest, in pages of 20 lines: <Return> to
	  continue, A to abort. The -a (address), -c (callsign), -r (reverse
	  order) and -l (count) options remain available. The connection to
	  the White Pages is closed before the display, so that a pause at
	  the prompt does not keep it open.

fpac (4.1.6-rc2)

	* fpacnode "Ports" command: new "Callsign" column between "Port"
	  and "Dev". It displays the callsign of each AX.25 port (axports)
	  and NetRom port (nrports), and the full ROSE address of the rose0
	  port (rsports), which has no callsign. Until now no fpacnode
	  command gave the callsign of the node's ports. The columns are now
	  aligned with spaces instead of tabs, so that the titles stay above
	  the values whatever the terminal or packet client, and leading
	  spaces in the descriptions no longer shift the display.
	* fpacnode "u" (Users) command: the "Port" column of level 2 AX.25
	  sessions always displayed the AX.25 port (axudp), because all
	  these sessions go through the same ax0 interface. NetRom links,
	  which use the callsign of a NetRom port (for example F6BVP-4), now
	  display that port (netnod), and ROSE links, which use the L3call
	  of the node, display the ROSE port (rose0). Only real AX.25
	  connections stay on the AX.25 port.
	* Source archive: the French release notes (NOUVELLES and
	  ChangeLog.fr) are now included in the tarball; they were missing
	  from it up to 4.1.6-rc1.

fpac (4.1.6-rc1)

	* ROSE connection: the "Relaying via ..." message displayed before
	  the connection sometimes named a neighbour that was not the one
	  actually used. The kernel first chooses a neighbour whose ROSE
	  link is already established (displayed "Opened" by the routes
	  command), and only falls back to a "Closed" neighbour if there is
	  none; FPAC simply named the first neighbour of the list, even
	  "Closed". The message now follows the kernel's rule exactly and
	  names the neighbour actually tried. A "Closed" neighbour remains
	  usable as a last resort: it is not excluded.
	* Automatic ROSE failover: the neighbour removed after a failure is
	  now chosen with the same rule as the kernel. Previously, if the
	  failed neighbour was not the first of the list, FPAC could remove
	  the route of another neighbour and retry the same failed neighbour
	  until the attempts ran out. The announcement of the neighbour of
	  the next attempt is fixed too. No "Relaying via" message is
	  displayed for a connection to the node itself (internal loopback
	  neighbour).
	* NetRom node list: the colour of the quality now shows whether the
	  node is really reachable, and not only its advertised quality, in
	  all three sort orders (by callsign, by alias, by quality) and in
	  "ne *". Red: quality 0, quality exactly 120 (frozen value from
	  rc.netrom, never refreshed), invalid active route, or route
	  neighbour whose link failures have reached the kernel limit
	  (link_fails_count), or whose AX.25 link is stuck awaiting
	  connection (SABM without answer; the kernel only counts the
	  failure after the ten attempts). Green: quality above 200 and
	  neighbour without failure. Yellow: the rest. The kernel silently
	  drops any packet to a node whose active route is out of range
	  ("w" column greater than "n" in /proc/net/nr_nodes), which happens
	  after link failures when the node has a single route: the table
	  then displays 247 to 255 but nothing is transmitted.
	* NetRom connection to such a node: the message "*** Invalid NetRom
	  route (active route 2 of 1): the kernel will not forward anything
	  to ..." is displayed instead of leaving the connection waiting
	  without explanation; a warning also reports an AX.25 link to the
	  neighbour stuck awaiting connection ("attempt 6/10, no answer");
	  "ne <node>" reports the invalid active route.
	* Message language: fpacnode now displays its connection and
	  routing messages in English or in French, about forty messages
	  for now ("Commands = ?" prompt, connection, failure, ROSE and
	  NetRom relaying, failover attempts, route or link warnings); the
	  other messages remain in English. The default language is the
	  system's, read from LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES, LANGUAGE or LANG and then
	  from /etc/default/locale and /etc/locale.conf (daemons started at
	  boot often have no language variable), or the one given by the
	  "Language = en | fr | auto" line of fpac.conf. The "LAng 1"
	  (English) or "LAng 2" (French) command changes the language of the
	  current session; the choice is not remembered. The messages added
	  in French in previous versions ("Relais via", "Route
	  indisponible"...) are now in English by default and translated for
	  French sessions.
	* wpedit: the confirmation messages (record updated, deleted,
	  restored) are now displayed immediately instead of staying
	  invisible in a buffer. Asking to delete a record that is already
	  deleted, or to restore a record that is not deleted, now displays
	  a simple message and returns to the menu; previously the server's
	  refusal could close the wpedit session.

-- Bernard Pidoux <bernard.f6bvp@gmail.com>  Saturday, september 19 2026

Assisted by Claude Sonnet 5 (Anthropic) <noreply@anthropic.com>.

fpac (4.1.5)

	* White Pages (network directory): several defects that prevented
	  records from synchronising correctly between nodes have been
	  fixed. A record marked "deleted" can again repair itself if the
	  originating node keeps announcing it, instead of staying stuck
	  until a manual intervention. Each node now re-announces its own
	  record regularly (every 7 days), even without a restart, so that
	  it never looks stale to the other nodes. The delay before a
	  deleted record is permanently erased is now respected. A deleted
	  record no longer appears in the user Wp command but remains
	  visible to the sysop through wplist. A successful explicit ROSE
	  connection to a callsign whose record was marked "deleted" now
	  restores it automatically. wplist displays a status (Ok/DELETED)
	  and a better layout; several wpedit commands that no longer
	  worked (record deletion in particular) are repaired, with a clear
	  message when the daemon is not running.

	* Reliability of the White Pages daemon (fpacwpd): several causes
	  of hangs or crashes have been fixed -- a connection to a
	  neighbouring node stuck while being established isolated the node
	  until a manual restart; the daemon could crash a few minutes after
	  starting; a deleted record that was too old, received from a
	  neighbour, cut the session instead of simply being ignored. All
	  this is now fixed, and the daemon recovers by itself in these
	  situations.

	* ROSE connections: the automatic failover is more robust and now
	  also covers the most frequent case (immediate failure of the
	  first attempt). The messages displayed to the user now name the
	  neighbour actually tried instead of staying vague. A short ROSE
	  address typed without the network (DNIC) was silently completed
	  with that of the local node, which could send the connection to
	  the wrong network without warning -- fixed. Connecting to an alias
	  now displays what it really corresponds to.

	* AX.25/NetRom connections: a connection attempt to an invalid or
	  unconfigured AX.25 port is now correctly rejected instead of being
	  silently accepted on the wrong port. The message displayed during
	  a connection attempt now gives the port used. "Connect CALLSIGN
	  via DIGIPEATER" (without giving a port) has been repaired; this
	  syntax was no longer interpreted correctly.

	* Display: the fpacnode colours can be customised without
	  recompiling through a configuration file
	  (/usr/local/etc/ax25/fpacnode.colors -- see that file for the
	  details of the settings). Several colour inconsistencies have
	  been fixed (users list, links, route quality). In the NetRom node
	  list ("ne"), a quality still frozen at its startup value is now
	  displayed in red to spot the entries never really confirmed by
	  the network. The node status ("s") clearly distinguishes the
	  declared user port from the port actually used by the current
	  session, and displays the exact build time in addition to the
	  date.

	* General stability: several crashes have been fixed, in particular
	  a systematic crash after the command sequence "s" then "u", buffer
	  overflows in the display and the messaging, and a sort of the
	  NetRom list that could loop forever on a large network.

	* Miscellaneous: the ChangeLog and the NEWS file are now also
	  available in French (ChangeLog.fr and NOUVELLES). Building the
	  source package (make dist) has been made reliable, and the build
	  now works correctly on 32-bit architectures (armhf).

